    @plan {
        goal
            A Rust language-server binary replaces the Langium process. Open buffers parse with reqlan-parse. Workspace names come from the ideas index. Closed files have no retained syntax tree.

        constraints
            - vscode-languageclient stays. Transport becomes stdio.
            - lsp-server and lsp-types only. No second parser.
            - Open-file links follow open_file_algorithm.
            - UTF-8 spans convert to UTF-16 LSP positions.
            - TextMate stays a checked-in grammar. The editor paints it before semantic tokens.
            - With the rust engine, non-.rq comment links come from the server. The node engine keeps the host provider.
            - Per-target VSIX ships bin/reqlan-lsp. The napi addon stays for the index host.
            - Default engine is rust. Node starts only as a fallback.

        steps
            1. Write this idea and point language_server and grammar_and_parser at it.
            2. Scaffold reqlan-lsp: initialize, didOpen, didChange, didClose, parse diagnostics.
            3. Launch it from the extension. Default is rust.
            4. Port open-file features: tokens, links, definition, hover, highlight, diagnostics.
            5. Read the ideas index for completion, rename, code actions, lenses, and inlays.
            6. Pack the binary in the VSIX matrix.
            7. Keep Langium only for host helpers. The server process does not load it when the engine is rust.

        open
            - Host move/delete/barrel planners still use Langium DI.
    }
}
